TD|For carotid sinus hypersensitivity in the studied patients.":["u114"],"c43-single-intervention-list|row:Vitamin D supplementation|TH|Vitamin D supplementation":["u115"],"c43-single-intervention-list|row:Vitamin D supplementation|TD|For people with low vitamin D levels living in residential aged care facilities.":["u116"],"falls-exercise|Exercise: preserve the population":["u22"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:Programme|TH|Programme":["u82"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:Programme|TH|Strategy":["u83"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:Individually prescribed home exercise|TH|Individually prescribed home exercise":["u84"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:Individually prescribed home exercise|TD|Three sessions per week: tandem standing and walking; toe and heel walking; walking backward, sideways and turning; stepping over objects; bending to pick up an object; home stair climbing; sit-to-stand; knee squats; moderate-intensity strengthening with ankle cuff weights; plus a walking programme.":["u85"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:LiFE|TH|LiFE":["u86"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:LiFE|TD|Integrate balance and strength training into daily activity whenever opportunities arise, rather than a scheduled exercise set. Reduce the base of support; move to limits of sway; shift weight between feet; step over objects; turn and change direction.":["u87"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:Hybrid home–group programme|TH|Hybrid home–group programme":["u88"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:Hybrid home–group programme|TD|Progressive, individually tailored group training for dynamic balance, strength, bone, endurance, flexibility, gait and functional skills, supplemented by home strength and balance exercises twice weekly.":["u89"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:Tai chi|TH|Tai chi":["u90"],"c43-exercise-programmes|row:Tai chi|TD|Group practice of controlled leaning, multidirectional weight shifting, body alignment awareness, and coordinated movement of the arms, legs and trunk.":["u91"],"copy|row:Programme and population|TH|Programme and population":["u92"],"copy|row:Programme and population|TH|Reported outcome":["u93"],"copy|row:Rapid stepping training: meta-analysis of seven RCTs|TH|Rapid stepping training: meta-analysis of seven RCTs":["u94"],"c43-stepping-comparison|row:Rapid stepping training: meta-analysis of seven RCTs|TD|52% reduction in falls.":["u95"],"copy|row:Home programme in older people recently discharged from hospital|TH|Home programme in older people recently discharged from hospital":["u96"],"c43-discharge-comparison|row:Home programme in older people recently discharged from hospital|TD|43% increase in falls in that programme.":["u97"],"falls-vision|Vision and spectacles":["u23"],"falls-vision-contrast|Vision interventions: surgery and refraction are not interchangeable":["u117"],"falls-vitamin-d|Vitamin D: community and residential care":["u24"],"falls-home-feet|Home safety and feet":["u25"],"falls-delivery|Multifactorial care and delivery":["u26"],"falls-reference|Intervention results at a glance":["u27"],"copy|row:Context|TH|Context":["u28"],"copy|row:Context|TH|Chapter finding":["u29"],"copy|row:Community exercise|TH|Community exercise":["u30"],"c43-reference-community|row:Community exercise|TD|Community-dwelling older people allocated to well-designed exercise programs had 23% fewer falls than those allocated to control programs.":["u31"],"copy|row:Recent hospital discharge|TH|Recent hospital discharge":["u32"],"c43-reference-discharge|row:Recent hospital discharge|TD|One home-based exercise program significantly increased falls in older people recently discharged from hospitals by 43%.":["u33"],"copy|row:Residential care|TH|Residential care":["u34"],"c43-reference-residential|row:Residential care|TD|A trial of twice-weekly combined progressive resistance and balance training for 25 weeks reduced falls by 55% in long-term aged-care residents.":["u35"],"copy|row:Multifactorial intervention|TH|Multifactorial intervention":["u36"],"c43-reference-multifactorial|row:Multifactorial intervention|TD|Multifactorial interventions reduced falls overall by 23% in community-dwellers.":["u37"],"copy|CHAPTER 43 / HAZZARD":["u38"],"c43-definition|A fall is defined as an event which results in a person coming to rest inadvertently on the ground or floor or other lower level.":["u39"],"c43-opening|Read the chapter by setting: who is falling, how risk is assessed, and which interventions were studied in that population.":["u40"],"c43-opening|Keep a risk association separate from an intervention result.":["u41"],"c43-community-incidence|Prospective studies in community settings report fall incidence rates of 32% to 40% in people aged 65 and older.":["u42"],"c43-community-incidence|Prospective studies in community settings report fall incidence rates of 40% to 50% in people beyond the age of 75.":["u43"],"c43-residential-incidence|Prospective studies in residential aged care facilities report fall incidence rates between 30% and 56%.":["u44"],"c43-major-injury|Major injuries are caused by 10% to 15% of falls.":["u45"],"c43-noninjurious-fall-consequences|Even without physical injury, falls can lead to fear of falling, reduced mobility, increased dependency and frailty through avoidance of daily activities.":["u118"],"c43-factor-map-guide|sensorimotor and balance systems can be impaired and predispose to falls (Figure 43-1).":["u274"],"c43-factor-map-guide|Table 43-1 summarizes the findings of numerous prospective cohort studies and rates each risk factor according to the strength of published evidence.":["u275"],"c43-risk-table-completeness|***Strong evidence (consistently found in high-quality studies); **moderate evidence (usually but not always found); *weak evidence (occasionally but not usually found); and - little or no evidence (not found in published studies despite research to examine the issue).":["u276"],"c43-association-cautions|Alcohol consumption has not been shown to be a fall-risk factor in several large cohort studies.":["u49"],"c43-association-cautions|Vestibular disorders and orthostatic hypotension have not been found to be strong risk factors for falls in community-based research studies.":["u50"],"c43-association-cautions|Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs do not increase fall risk in an adjusted analysis.":["u51"],"c43-alcohol-and-injury|Traumatic brain injuries are twice as prevalent among older people presenting with an indication of alcohol use prior to the fall.":["u52"],"c43-chronic-alcohol-consequences|Long-term high alcohol intake can cause cerebellar atrophy, peripheral neuropathy and osteoporosis.":["u119"],"c43-chronic-alcohol-consequences|These consequences are distinct from the chapter’s observational findings about alcohol use and fall incidence.":["u120"],"c43-psychoactive-risks|The use of psychoactive medications leads to a two to threefold increased risk of falls.":["u53"],"c43-psychoactive-risks|The use of psychoactive medications leads to a twofold increased risk of experiencing a hip fracture.":["u54"],"c43-antihypertensive-window|The risk of falls is significantly increased within the 24-hour periods following the initiation of an antihypertensive or change in antihypertensive dose.":["u55"],"c43-withdrawal-framing|The risk estimates and the withdrawal studies answer different questions.":["u56"],"c43-withdrawal-framing|Keep the intervention result attached to its follow-up and the medications studied.":["u57"],"c43-withdrawal-evidence|Fall-risk inducing drugs withdrawal strategies mostly targeting centrally acting medications did not significantly reduce fall rates in older people over a 6- to 12-month follow-up period.":["u58"],"c43-timed-up-and-go|The Timed Up and Go Test involves measuring the time taken for a person to rise from a chair, walk 3 meters at normal pace, turn, return to the chair, and sit down.":["u59"],"c43-timed-up-and-go|The predictive value of the Timed Up and Go test for falls in community-dwelling older adults is limited and no cut-point for impaired performance can be recommended.":["u60"],"c43-assessment-alternatives|Simple alternatives to the TUG with good predictive accuracy for falls in the chapter include the Alternate Step Test, Sit to Stand Test and Short Physical Performance Battery.":["u121"],"c43-hospital-assessment|Hospital patients aged 65 and older should be assumed to be at risk of falls and automatically undergo multifactorial assessments and interventions.":["u61"],"c43-hospital-assessment|Hospital patients aged 50 to 64 who have an underlying condition that places them at risk should be assumed to be at risk of falls and automatically undergo multifactorial assessments.":["u62"],"c43-single-intervention-scope|The chapter enumerates seven single interventions shown to reduce falls in RCTs.":["u122"],"c43-single-intervention-scope|Retain their patient selection and setting; this is an evidence inventory, not a prescription to give every intervention to every older person.":["u123"],"c43-community-exercise|Community-dwelling older people allocated to well-designed exercise programs had 23% fewer falls than those allocated to control programs.":["u63"],"c43-community-exercise|Exercise programs that provide a high challenge to balance and three or more hours per week of prescribed exercise show greater effects in preventing falls.":["u64"],"c43-exercise-setting|The next findings concern different populations.":["u65"],"c43-exercise-setting|They should be read as separate results rather than combined into a single promise about exercise.":["u66"],"c43-exercise-limitations|Exercise interventions do not reduce fall rates among long-term stroke survivors.":["u67"],"c43-exercise-limitations|Exercise interventions do not reduce fall rates among older people recently discharged from hospital.":["u68"],"c43-post-discharge-trial|One home-based exercise program significantly increased falls in older people recently discharged from hospitals by 43%.":["u69"],"c43-rapid-stepping|Rapid stepping training, aimed at improving a key balance-recovery response, reduced falls by 52% in a meta-analysis of seven randomised controlled trials.":["u98"],"c43-oldest-exercise|Exercise reduces falls even in subgroups aged 75 years and older, whether delivered individually or in groups, by a health professional or a trained exercise leader.":["u99"],"c43-residential-exercise|A trial of twice-weekly combined progressive resistance and balance training for 25 weeks reduced falls by 55% in long-term aged-care residents.":["u70"],"c43-cataract-first-eye|Two trials examined expedited cataract surgery.":["u124"],"c43-cataract-first-eye|In the first-eye trial, the operated group had a 34% reduction in fall rate compared with control; the difference was statistically significant.":["u125"],"c43-cataract-second-eye|The second-eye trial found a similar 32% reduction, but it did not reach statistical significance.":["u126"],"c43-cataract-second-eye|Similar effect estimates do not establish equally certain benefit.":["u127"],"c43-vision-intervention-trials|Evidence for updating glasses is less clear.":["u128"],"c43-vision-intervention-trials|One RCT of a vision-improvement intervention in people with impaired vision did not significantly reduce falls.":["u129"],"c43-vision-intervention-trials|A second RCT assessing vision and providing vision-related treatments significantly increased fall rate by about 50%.":["u130"],"c43-cataract-refraction-cohort|A prospective cohort associated cataract surgery with fewer falls when spectacle-lens power updates of the operated eye were cautious—less than ±0.75 dioptre change.":["u131"],"c43-cataract-refraction-cohort|This observational condition does not turn every refractive update into a fall-prevention intervention.":["u132"],"c43-vision-interventions|A recent prospective cohort study found that cataract surgery is associated with fewer falls in older adults, providing spectacle lens power updates of the operated eye are cautious (less than +/-0.75 diopter change).":["u144"],"c43-single-lens-distance|Providing single lens distance glasses to community-dwelling older people at risk of falls who regularly used multifocal glasses outdoors was effective in significantly reducing all falls by 40%.":["u145"],"c43-vitamin-d-settings|Vitamin D supplementation does not improve physical function or prevent falls in community-dwelling older adults, even in those with vitamin D deficiency.":["u73"],"c43-vitamin-d-settings|Vitamin D supplementation may reduce falls in older adults living in residential aged care facilities.":["u74"],"c43-home-safety|Intensive home safety interventions carried out by occupational therapists can reduce fall risk by 21% overall.":["u75"],"c43-home-safety|Intensive home safety interventions carried out by occupational therapists can reduce fall risk by 39% for high-risk groups.":["u76"],"c43-podiatry|Multifaceted podiatry interventions can reduce falls by 23% in older people living in the community.":["u77"],"c43-podiatry-bundle|The podiatry bundle includes footwear advice, foot and ankle exercises, routine podiatry, and provision of foot orthoses and/or new safe footwear.":["u133"],"c43-multifactorial-result|Multifactorial interventions reduced falls overall by 23% in community-dwellers.":["u78"],"c43-hospital-multifactorial-members|Successful hospital-inpatient multifactorial programmes combine supervised exercise and balance training, resident and staff education, medication review, vitamin D supplementation, environmental review, walking aids and provision of hip protectors.":["u134"],"c43-hospital-multifactorial-members|The programme result is not proof that every component independently reduces falls.":["u135"],"c43-racf-multifaceted-programme|One RACF multifaceted programme combined staff training and feedback, resident information and education, environmental adaptations, hip protectors, and twice-weekly exercise in groups of six to eight led by exercise instructors.":["u136"],"c43-racf-multifaceted-programme|It reduced falls by 45%; the effect belongs to the bundle in that setting.":["u137"],"c43-direct-delivery|Multiple risk factor interventions are more effective in reducing falls if the interventions are provided directly rather than relying on referral to routine service providers.":["u79"],"c43-reference-guide|Use this table to keep the setting beside the result when revising.":["u80"],"c43-reference-guide|The study descriptions remain part of each finding.":["u81"],"copy|Gillespie LD, Robertson MC, Gillespie WJ, et al. Interventions for preventing falls in older people living in the community. Cochrane Database Syst Rev . 2012;9: CD007146.":["u327"],"copy|Hewitt J, Goodall S, Clemson L, et al. Progressive resistance and balance training for falls prevention in long-term residential aged care: a cluster randomized trial of the Sunbeam program. J Am Med Dir Assoc . 2018; 19:361.":["u327"],"copy|Hopewell S, Copsey B, Nicolson P, et al. Multifactorial interventions for preventing falls in older people living in the community: a systematic review and meta-analysis of 41 trials and almost 20,000 participants. Br J Sports Med . 2020;54:1340.":["u327"],"copy|Lamb SE, Bruce J, Hossain A, et al. Prevention of fall injury trial study group. Screening and intervention to prevent falls and fractures in older people. N Engl J Med . 2020;383:1848.":["u327"],"copy|Lamb SE, Jorstad-Stein EC, Hauer K, et al. Development of a common outcome data set for fall injury prevention trials: the Prevention of Falls Network Europe consensus. J Am Geriatr Soc . 2005;53:1618.":["u327"],"copy|Lee J, Negm A, Wong E, et al. Does deprescribing fall-associated drugs reduce falls and its complications? A systematic review. Innov Aging . 2017;1:268.":["u327"],"copy|The association between the use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and falls is":["u377"],"copy|Table 43-1 — Risk factors for falls":["u377"]},"preservedBreaks":{"Additionally, older women are also more likely to sustain fall-related fractures due to a higher prevalence of osteoporosis.":[46],"The association between the use of nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and falls is inconsistent across studies but the most recent and comprehensive systematic review found that these medications do not increase fall risk in an adjusted analysis.":[84],"Systematic review findings indicate that while exercise interventions are effective among people with Parkinson disease and those with cognitive impairment, they do not reduce fall rates among long-term stroke survivors or older people recently discharged from hospital.":[89],"A meta-analysis showed that intensive interventions carried out by occupational therapists can reduce fall risk by 21% overall and by 39% for high-risk groups.":[90]},"sequenceOffsets":[[292,252],[293,-1],[328,0],[357,2],[358,-1],[360,0],[377,4],[378,0],[381,1],[458,7],[459,0],[465,1],[490,9],[491,0],[499,1]]}
